Signal-Server takes a one-time EC pre-key with a query for the first key and a DeleteItem
returning ALL_OLD (`SingleUseECPreKeyStore.take`). Whoever gets the old item back has the key,
which relies on DynamoDB applying the delete atomically. Real DynamoDB does; DynamoDB Local
doesn't. Two simultaneous deletes of one item both get it back, so two senders fetching a
bundle at once get the same one-time key, and the second session's pre-key message fails to
decrypt (pending/tour.md, finding 17). DynamoDB Local's conditional writes are atomic, so the
delete here only goes through while the key exists, and a delete that loses counts as no key,
as an empty ALL_OLD does: `take` moves on to the next one. Against real DynamoDB this changes
nothing.

